1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,280
A mysterious explosion beneath Manhattan drives New Yorkers to the brink of panic.

2
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:12,160
A Minneapolis bridge crowded with cars abruptly collapses.

3
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:17,800
Tiny particles trigger a helicopter crash that kills 46 people.

4
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:24,380
And did natural gas drillers unleash a torrent of hot mud that destroyed a city?

5
00:00:24,381 --> 00:00:30,380
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:43,640
It started with a roar.

7
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:48,120
Then, the Earth opened up and a powerful force emerged,

8
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:52,360
propelling a violent torrent of blistering mud, rock, and steam

9
00:00:52,361 --> 00:00:53,980
80 stories into the sky.

10
00:00:54,380 --> 00:00:57,460
That is like the pit of hell.

11
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:00,880
This engineering disaster struck the heart of New York City.

12
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:07,560
The explosion occurred at 6 p.m. on July 18, 2007.

13
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:14,500
It erupted through the asphalt at Lexington Avenue and 41st Street in Midtown Manhattan.

14
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:21,780
The blast tore a huge crater in the middle of the block and blew out windows in the surrounding buildings.

15
00:01:21,781 --> 00:01:28,120
The black sky and falling debris triggered memories of the 2001 terrorist attacks.

16
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:32,400
When we first heard it, we thought the explosion was in Grand Central Station.

17
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:35,820
And we also heard the word massive explosion,

18
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:38,480
and obviously we were concerned at what that might be.

19
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:42,080
Officials quickly allayed the public's worst fears.

20
00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:46,820
There is no reason to believe that there was any terrorism involved whatsoever.

21
00:01:46,821 --> 00:01:50,980
It is probably just a failure of part of our infrastructure.

22
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:55,700
One person died from shock, and 44 others were treated for injuries.

23
00:01:56,580 --> 00:02:00,360
The disaster was the result of an underground steam pipe explosion.

24
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:14,895
The impact of something like this steam explosion, the consequences could be very similar to the impacts of a break in any infrastructure if there was a terrorist

25
00:02:14,896 --> 00:02:15,596
attack.

26
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:17,780
I think we have a lot to learn from this.

27
00:02:17,781 --> 00:02:26,280
The streets of Manhattan rest above an intricate underworld that includes electrical lines, plumbing lines, subways and sewers.

28
00:02:28,620 --> 00:02:31,260
The steam pipe system is built into this maze.

29
00:02:32,340 --> 00:02:39,600
The pipes are generally four feet below street level, but sometimes to accommodate other utility lines, they go deeper to 10 feet.

30
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:42,820
And some steam mains plunge 20 feet underground.

31
00:02:42,821 --> 00:02:50,160
steam delivery pipes have been part of the island's infrastructure since 1881.

32
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:55,920
That year, the New York Steam Company began laying pipes in the streets

33
00:02:55,921 --> 00:03:00,460
to supply steam for locomotives, heating, cooking, and other power.

34
00:03:00,940 --> 00:03:06,600
There was a building in Lower Manhattan that actually constructed 24 small boilers

35
00:03:06,601 --> 00:03:09,860
and erected about three miles worth of steam piping

36
00:03:09,861 --> 00:03:12,920
to serve an area down around the Wall Street area.

37
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:16,660
New York's steam business continued to grow.

38
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,240
And today, it is part of Con Edison, a private utility company.

39
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:28,420
It produces the steam at seven plants, including the East River Generating Station.

40
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:35,580
Though it may seem outdated, steam power is still an efficient and cost-effective choice for high-rises.

41
00:03:35,581 --> 00:03:42,580
And Con Ed provides steam for hot water, heating and air conditioning to over 1,800 buildings

42
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:48,980
in New York City, including the United Nations Headquarters and the Empire State Building.

43
00:03:48,981 --> 00:03:54,893
The Con Ed steam system is comprised of approximately 105 miles of steam piping.

44
00:03:54,894 --> 00:03:55,780
Steam piping

45
00:03:55,781 --> 00:04:00,551
ranges in size from 2 inches in diameter all the way up to 36 inches in diameter.

46
00:04:00,552 --> 00:04:01,252
The system

47
00:04:01,253 --> 00:04:05,846
itself produces roughly 27 billion pounds of steam a year.

48
00:04:05,847 --> 00:04:08,280
Our peak load day is somewhere

49
00:04:08,281 --> 00:04:14,500
just under 11 million pounds an hour of steam a day during the winter months, and in the

50
00:04:14,501 --> 00:04:18,340
summer months we could actually see loads as high as 8 million pounds an hour of steam.

51
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:26,200
Immediately after the steam pipe explosion, Con Edison went to work to assess and repair

52
00:04:26,201 --> 00:04:31,178
the damage, the cause of the explosion remained a mystery.

53
00:04:31,179 --> 00:04:34,440
The steam pipe that burst was 83 years

54
00:04:34,441 --> 00:04:38,188
old, but that by itself didn't explain its failure.

55
00:04:38,189 --> 00:04:40,760
Some experts suspected that factors

56
00:04:40,761 --> 00:04:44,912
in the pipe's subterranean environment were to blame.

57
00:04:44,913 --> 00:04:48,280
The vibration in the streets, the fact that

58
00:04:48,281 --> 00:04:54,640
there are electric currents that erode the structure of a pipe, the fact that there's a lot

59
00:04:54,641 --> 00:05:00,620
of construction going on that moves the soil that's needed to support pipes like this.

60
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:09,040
All of these factors, water, salt, infiltrating the underground, will undermine the age.

61
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:14,060
Other experts believe that the explosion was caused by something known in the steam business

62
00:05:14,061 --> 00:05:16,240
as a water hammer.

63
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:24,200
A classic case of water hammer in steam pipes is where the pipes have been shut down for

64
00:05:24,201 --> 00:05:29,120
some reason, the steam starts to cool and so that means the water begins to

65
00:05:29,121 --> 00:05:33,420
condense out of that steam. Well those little beads of water gather on the

66
00:05:33,421 --> 00:05:36,420
inside of the steam pipe and they roll down to the bottom and pretty soon

67
00:05:36,421 --> 00:05:41,140
there's a pool of water. Steam travels through the underground pipes at 75

68
00:05:41,141 --> 00:05:45,620
miles per hour. If the water in the pipe has not been drained before the steam is

69
00:05:45,621 --> 00:05:51,240
turned on, the steam will pick it up and propel it. When the water hits a turn or

70
00:05:51,241 --> 00:05:58,760
the end of a pipe, it has enough force to burst through the metal.

71
00:05:58,761 --> 00:06:03,320
As Con Edison continues its investigation, it has not yet disclosed whether or not the

72
00:06:03,321 --> 00:06:06,700
ruptured pipe was near a turn.

73
00:06:06,701 --> 00:06:13,220
But it has acknowledged that the rupture in the pipe measured 5.5 feet long.

74
00:06:13,221 --> 00:06:17,080
The East River Steam Plant uses high-tech methods to regulate the steam distribution

75
00:06:17,081 --> 00:06:18,080
in the city.

76
00:06:18,081 --> 00:06:24,340
They monitor the temperature and pressure of the steam in an effort to prevent disasters like the explosion from occurring.

77
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:31,760
Within three days of the disaster, part of the street where the explosion happened have been reopened.

78
00:06:32,900 --> 00:06:36,780
One month later, some of the windows in the surrounding buildings have been replaced.

79
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:43,580
The exact cause of the steam pipe disaster remains unknown and is still under investigation.

80
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:46,340
The biggest blessing is we had so few injuries.

81
00:06:46,341 --> 00:06:47,840
That, I think, is the biggest blessing.

82
00:06:48,220 --> 00:06:52,720
The other is that we were able to get so many response personnel here,

83
00:06:53,420 --> 00:06:55,140
care for people very quickly,

84
00:06:55,460 --> 00:07:00,020
assure people that this was not anything other than a terrible accident.

85
00:07:01,240 --> 00:07:05,560
While the steam pipe explosion in New York may be a result of an aging infrastructure,

86
00:07:06,860 --> 00:07:11,860
another large city on the eastern seaboard experienced an engineering disaster

87
00:07:11,861 --> 00:07:13,760
from a much more recent construction.

88
00:07:16,340 --> 00:07:24,580
In 1991, the Massachusetts Turnpike Authority created the Central Artery Project to ease the gridlocked traffic in Boston.

89
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:28,460
It became affectionately known as the Big Dig.

90
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:32,780
Part of the massive construction project was the Ted Williams Tunnel.

91
00:07:34,680 --> 00:07:40,780
Completed in 1995, it spanned 1.6 miles, linking downtown Boston to Logan Airport.

92
00:07:40,781 --> 00:07:48,660
to link the tunnel to the i-90 freeway contractors began building a second connector tunnel

93
00:07:48,661 --> 00:07:53,840
called the d street portal it officially opened in 2005

94
00:07:53,841 --> 00:08:01,400
on july 10 2006 a passenger car was traveling eastbound through the d street portal

95
00:08:01,401 --> 00:08:07,880
as the vehicle approached the end of the tunnel several large panels of the suspended concrete

96
00:08:07,881 --> 00:08:10,680
ceiling, detached abruptly from their anchors.

97
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:16,080
26 tons of concrete and suspension hardware fell,

98
00:08:16,740 --> 00:08:17,520
crushing the car.

99
00:08:19,420 --> 00:08:22,440
Melena Del Valle, a 38-year-old mother of three,

100
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:23,360
was fatally injured.

101
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:26,900
The tunnel was immediately closed,

102
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:29,160
and tens of thousands of vehicles per day

103
00:08:29,161 --> 00:08:30,240
had to be rerouted.

104
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:33,080
What happened last night, of course, is unacceptable.

105
00:08:33,540 --> 00:08:36,240
People should not have to drive through the turnpike tunnels

106
00:08:36,241 --> 00:08:38,160
with their fingers crossed.

107
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:42,400
Investigators from the National Transportation Safety Board

108
00:08:42,401 --> 00:08:43,780
were called to the scene

109
00:08:43,781 --> 00:08:45,880
and began sifting through the rubble,

110
00:08:46,300 --> 00:08:47,800
looking for the cause of this disaster.

111
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:50,920
If these panels could fall,

112
00:08:51,340 --> 00:08:53,360
were all the tunnels potential death traps?

113
00:08:54,580 --> 00:08:58,320
Why did this 40-by-30-foot section

114
00:08:58,321 --> 00:09:02,700
of suspended ceiling come down and strike this car?

115
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:04,780
Since the ceiling was suspended,

116
00:09:05,540 --> 00:09:08,680
investigators started looking at the configuration of the hardware used

117
00:09:08,681 --> 00:09:11,920
to hold each of the 4,600-pound concrete blocks in place.

118
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:19,100
During construction, the contractors had first drilled holes in the concrete roof of the tunnel.

119
00:09:20,460 --> 00:09:25,000
Then, brackets had been bolted into the holes, secured with epoxy adhesive.

120
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:31,660
Metal hangers attached to the brackets held up the concrete panels, forming a drop ceiling.

121
00:09:31,661 --> 00:09:38,940
NTSB investigators found that of the 600 steel anchors used to hold up this section of the

122
00:09:38,941 --> 00:09:44,980
suspended ceiling, more than 20 had failed, and others had been dangerously dislodged.

123
00:09:45,640 --> 00:09:48,240
Could an error have been made in the installation procedure?

124
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:52,910
Each of those anchors was proof tested after it was installed.

125
00:09:52,911 --> 00:09:54,960
In other words, they drill the hole,

126
00:09:55,060 --> 00:10:00,220
they put the anchor in, they let the epoxy cure, and then they come back several days later,

127
00:10:00,221 --> 00:10:05,920
and they're able to actually gauge how much force that they're using to pull down on this anchor.

128
00:10:06,420 --> 00:10:12,560
The mystery deepened as the NTSB discovered that the anchors had all passed the contractor's epoxy proof tests.

129
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:17,740
So investigators turned their attention to the epoxy fastener itself.

130
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:20,280
They discovered a disturbing clue.

131
00:10:21,060 --> 00:10:24,680
It had been tested for strength, but not for endurance over time.

132
00:10:24,681 --> 00:10:30,980
You know, you don't build a tunnel with the expectation that you're just going to run traffic through there for a day or a week.

133
00:10:31,160 --> 00:10:37,600
You're looking at a long-term investment in a facility that you expect to have in service over a period of time.

134
00:10:39,480 --> 00:10:46,560
The investigators began to study what engineers call the creep factor in the epoxy material used to hold the anchors in place.

135
00:10:46,561 --> 00:10:52,820
Now by creep behavior, what you mean is if you take any plastic and you fix it at one

136
00:10:52,821 --> 00:10:55,960
end and apply a load at the other end, of course it will stretch.

137
00:10:55,961 --> 00:11:00,000
And if it's very stiff like this, it won't stretch very much, but you can measure how

138
00:11:00,001 --> 00:11:02,240
far that stretches.

139
00:11:02,241 --> 00:11:06,260
If you leave it like that and come back tomorrow, what you find is it's actually stretched a

140
00:11:06,261 --> 00:11:07,920
little further.

141
00:11:07,921 --> 00:11:12,360
If you come back a month from now or a year from now, it will stretch even further.

142
00:11:12,361 --> 00:11:17,420
And that additional extension as a function of time under load is called creep.

143
00:11:17,421 --> 00:11:22,600
By analyzing the actual bolts that were secured in the concrete at the scene of the disaster,

144
00:11:22,601 --> 00:11:26,480
scientists did find visual evidence of creep.

145
00:11:26,481 --> 00:11:30,640
Telltale signs that the epoxy had pulled away from the concrete surface within the anchor

146
00:11:30,641 --> 00:11:32,940
holes.

147
00:11:32,941 --> 00:11:35,700
These two anchors were from the failure area.

148
00:11:35,701 --> 00:11:40,780
And you can see that this one shows evidence of an adhesive failure, a failure between

149
00:11:40,781 --> 00:11:46,660
the surface of the epoxy and the surface of the hole in the concrete.

150
00:11:46,661 --> 00:11:51,320
From that evidence, we basically focused on looking at a time-dependent failure mechanism,

151
00:11:51,321 --> 00:11:52,320
which is the creep.

152
00:11:52,321 --> 00:11:58,280
While the NTSB investigators continued to dig for more clues, scientists at the National

153
00:11:58,281 --> 00:12:02,580
Institute for Standards and Technology were looking more deeply into the properties of

154
00:12:02,581 --> 00:12:04,900
the epoxies used in the tunnel.

155
00:12:04,901 --> 00:12:11,700
There were actually two types of epoxy, the standard set and the fast set, which dried

156
00:12:11,701 --> 00:12:12,700
more quickly.

157
00:12:12,701 --> 00:12:18,760
Well, NTSB provided us with fresh samples of two different epoxies, one labeled epoxy

158
00:12:18,761 --> 00:12:20,277
A and one labeled epoxy B.

159
00:12:20,278 --> 00:12:23,660
And they wanted us to do some basic characterizations, and

160
00:12:23,661 --> 00:12:27,300
they were particularly interested in creep behavior.

161
00:12:27,301 --> 00:12:34,200
NIST scientists prepared hardened samples of both types of epoxy, then loaded them

162
00:12:34,201 --> 00:12:39,840
separately into a rheometric systems analyzer, a flow measurement system.

163
00:12:39,841 --> 00:12:46,340
Then we start the test machine and it automatically does short-term experiments at a whole series

164
00:12:46,341 --> 00:12:52,500
of different temperatures. So the whole process is completely automated.

165
00:12:52,501 --> 00:12:58,140
After analyzing all the properties of the epoxies over six months, NIST scientists were able

166
00:12:58,141 --> 00:13:02,240
to determine that the fast-set epoxy had a considerably higher rate of creep.

167
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:06,860
Essentially, the higher it is, the softer it is.

168
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:11,220
So you can see epoxy A is getting soft much faster than epoxy B.

169
00:13:11,700 --> 00:13:14,340
What that means is it's deforming much more rapidly.

170
00:13:15,580 --> 00:13:19,040
The analysis solved the mystery of why the panels had fallen.

171
00:13:20,840 --> 00:13:24,060
The fast-set epoxy had been used to fasten the anchor bolts

172
00:13:24,061 --> 00:13:26,680
into the concrete roof of the I-90 connector tunnel.

173
00:13:28,140 --> 00:13:34,540
Because of its creep threshold, the fast set was unable to hold the bolts in place over a long period of time.

174
00:13:36,540 --> 00:13:40,080
The epoxy failed just one year after it had been installed.

175
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:46,680
If this module hadn't have failed, it would have been just a matter of time before another one would have failed.

176
00:13:46,840 --> 00:13:50,000
I mean, there was no doubt something was going to come down there.

177
00:13:50,520 --> 00:13:54,940
The D Street portal reopened five months after the disaster.

178
00:13:55,780 --> 00:13:58,180
The panels that fell were never replaced.

179
00:14:00,860 --> 00:14:07,820
The cost of the epoxy used in the D Street portal construction was $1,287.60.

180
00:14:08,440 --> 00:14:11,980
The price tag for redesigning, repairing, and inspecting all the tunnels

181
00:14:11,981 --> 00:14:16,640
after the ceiling collapse in the D Street connector reached $54 million.

182
00:14:17,960 --> 00:14:20,840
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

183
00:14:20,841 --> 00:14:30,320
On August 2, 2007, the city of Minneapolis, Minnesota suddenly became a city in crisis.

184
00:14:33,900 --> 00:14:43,494
A 500-foot span of the Interstate 35 highway, known as Bridge 9340, collapsed into the Mississippi River during the 6 p.m.

185
00:14:43,495 --> 00:14:44,280
rush hour.

186
00:14:44,281 --> 00:14:48,900
The bridge had been packed with cars and trucks.

187
00:14:49,640 --> 00:14:51,320
Nine people plunged to their deaths.

188
00:14:56,300 --> 00:15:01,280
Some people in their vehicles clung precariously on to what was left of the concrete bridge decking.

189
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,240
Waiting, stranded and scared, for help to arrive.

190
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:09,780
No one knew what could have caused such a disaster.

191
00:15:10,740 --> 00:15:14,620
The first thought was the tragedy, the human aspects of this.

192
00:15:15,020 --> 00:15:19,800
The second thing was, in an unfortunate way, it wasn't surprising to me

193
00:15:19,801 --> 00:15:23,300
because we have such a large infrastructure in the country today

194
00:15:23,301 --> 00:15:26,020
that things are bound to fail.

195
00:15:26,240 --> 00:15:31,420
There's just so many bridges, so many dams, so many airports in the country today

196
00:15:31,421 --> 00:15:34,240
that it's hard to keep everything running perfectly.

197
00:15:36,200 --> 00:15:38,580
Intense rescue efforts continued through the night.

198
00:15:39,240 --> 00:15:44,420
The next day, President George W. Bush visited the disaster site.

199
00:15:45,080 --> 00:15:50,120
I have met with the chief of police and the sheriff and rescue workers,

200
00:15:50,560 --> 00:15:54,900
people who represent men and women who are working as hard as they possibly can

201
00:15:54,901 --> 00:16:00,860
to save life and to find life, to go under these murky waters to find the facts.

202
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:06,020
What caused this 40-year-old bridge to suddenly collapse?

203
00:16:06,021 --> 00:16:09,620
Could the solution be found in the backstory of its construction?

204
00:16:11,540 --> 00:16:17,840
It was built as a result of the Federal Aid Highway Act, championed by President Dwight Eisenhower in 1956.

205
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:22,260
This act created America's interstate highway system.

206
00:16:23,560 --> 00:16:28,220
This wasn't just a bridge. It was a link in a mighty chain.

207
00:16:29,340 --> 00:16:34,300
Tens of thousands of bridges across 46,000 miles.

208
00:16:36,020 --> 00:16:46,040
Even though it looks so tragic now, it was part of the last great civil engineering effort in the United States.

209
00:16:46,860 --> 00:16:53,140
The Minnesota I-35 bridge was the last of the steel truss bridges, built as part of the interstate network.

210
00:16:54,780 --> 00:17:01,940
Trusses were very common in the eastern part of the country because concrete is not as readily available in the eastern half of the United States as it is out here.

211
00:17:01,941 --> 00:17:06,300
steel is more readily available and that's where all the industrialization went on back east with

212
00:17:06,301 --> 00:17:07,840
the steel mills and so forth.

213
00:17:07,841 --> 00:17:11,240
So a lot of steel trusses were built late in the 19th century in

214
00:17:11,241 --> 00:17:13,017
the first half of the 20th.

215
00:17:13,018 --> 00:17:17,560
Could an inherent flaw in truss bridge design explain the disaster in

216
00:17:17,561 --> 00:17:18,715
Minneapolis?

217
00:17:18,716 --> 00:17:25,740
To construct America's truss bridges, engineers used straight steel beams

218
00:17:25,741 --> 00:17:29,780
and configured them into a triangular pattern called the truss.

219
00:17:31,940 --> 00:17:37,460
The beams were secured with gusset plates that were riveted at the key joints where the individual pieces came together.

220
00:17:39,380 --> 00:17:41,860
The trusses rested on concrete piers.

221
00:17:43,220 --> 00:17:52,680
In Minneapolis, the concrete roadway deck lay on two parallel trusses that spanned the Mississippi and part of the riverbank on both the north and south sides.

222
00:17:54,260 --> 00:17:59,280
The same steel truss design was used on the Riverside Bridge in Los Angeles, California.

223
00:18:00,020 --> 00:18:01,760
It's been standing since 1939.

224
00:18:01,940 --> 00:18:06,620
These are all individual straight steel members.

225
00:18:06,980 --> 00:18:09,120
That's all this bridge is composed of, this truss.

226
00:18:09,820 --> 00:18:12,880
So let's start at the support over here, the very end of the bridge.

227
00:18:13,120 --> 00:18:15,780
There's a vertical member, there's a diagonal member,

228
00:18:16,020 --> 00:18:18,180
and there's a horizontal, the bottom cord member.

229
00:18:18,300 --> 00:18:23,120
And they all come together, so you have to fasten those three members together.

230
00:18:24,020 --> 00:18:27,120
The steel truss design is called non-redundant.

231
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:33,520
This means that if one of the horizontal, diagonal, or vertical steel members is cracked or damaged in some way,

232
00:18:34,060 --> 00:18:35,840
it will affect the entire structure.

233
00:18:37,860 --> 00:18:40,080
That doesn't mean it's unsafe.

234
00:18:40,580 --> 00:18:44,480
There are thousands of non-redundant bridges around the country,

235
00:18:44,720 --> 00:18:48,580
but it does mean you have to take an extra effort in the inspection.

236
00:18:50,720 --> 00:18:53,860
Inspectors examine the I-35 bridge every two years.

237
00:18:53,861 --> 00:18:57,420
could they have missed a crucial sign that disaster was imminent?

238
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:05,500
After its last inspection, it had been rated structurally deficient.

239
00:19:06,140 --> 00:19:09,460
But that wasn't an indication that the bridge could fall at any moment.

240
00:19:10,580 --> 00:19:13,960
There are currently over 1,600 bridges across America

241
00:19:13,961 --> 00:19:16,260
that are also rated structurally deficient.

242
00:19:16,800 --> 00:19:18,540
And none of them have been closed.

243
00:19:18,541 --> 00:19:25,540
Since there was construction on the bridge at the time of the collapse, some wonder if

244
00:19:25,541 --> 00:19:30,820
the extra weight on the deck might have strained the supports.

245
00:19:30,821 --> 00:19:35,360
But many suspect that the most probable cause of the collapse was that the bridge's steel

246
00:19:35,361 --> 00:19:42,580
had been weakened by so-called fatigue cracks caused by the ravages of time or the elements.

247
00:19:42,581 --> 00:19:44,720
Many of these cracks can be microscopic.

248
00:19:44,721 --> 00:19:50,600
It's not just the crack itself, it's the possibility that water will get in there or ice will get

249
00:19:50,601 --> 00:19:54,080
in there and work the crack over time.

250
00:19:54,081 --> 00:20:01,680
So just a hairline crack in a particularly bad place can cause a catastrophic failure.

251
00:20:01,681 --> 00:20:02,680
It's like a paper clip.

252
00:20:02,681 --> 00:20:06,220
If you bend the paper clip once, you can't just break it apart.

253
00:20:06,221 --> 00:20:12,360
But if you bend it over and over again, eventually the material cracks and then that crack grows

254
00:20:12,361 --> 00:20:14,440
and then it comes apart.

255
00:20:14,441 --> 00:20:19,440
Some experts blame a more surprising factor, the pigeons, who frequently perched on the

256
00:20:19,441 --> 00:20:21,400
bridge's steel beams.

257
00:20:21,401 --> 00:20:25,620
The bird's waste could clog the structure's drains and corrode its steel.

258
00:20:25,621 --> 00:20:31,940
Pigeons, as small as they are, can be a big problem because of their droppings.

259
00:20:31,941 --> 00:20:38,200
The ammonia chemically combines into a corrosive compound.

260
00:20:38,201 --> 00:20:42,080
Engineers explored methods to prevent the pigeons from landing on the bridge.

261
00:20:42,081 --> 00:20:47,640
The other thing, maybe most interesting, is they put screens over all those little holes

262
00:20:47,641 --> 00:20:51,080
in the box beams, those girders, to keep the pigeons out.

263
00:20:51,380 --> 00:20:55,320
So they wouldn't have a happy home in bridge 9340.

264
00:20:57,540 --> 00:21:04,780
It could be that the load on the bridge imposed by the construction work was one contributor.

265
00:21:04,781 --> 00:21:10,640
It's also possible that the pigeon waste could have also contributed to the problem.

266
00:21:10,641 --> 00:21:15,300
So it wouldn't surprise me if this fracture-critical bridge

267
00:21:15,301 --> 00:21:19,600
was really susceptible and failed because of a combination of factors,

268
00:21:19,601 --> 00:21:22,420
not just one silver bullet itself.

269
00:21:23,680 --> 00:21:26,280
While on-scene investigators work day and night

270
00:21:26,281 --> 00:21:28,300
to uncover clues to the bridge's collapse,

271
00:21:29,080 --> 00:21:31,840
engineers are seeking ways to prevent any similar disaster

272
00:21:31,841 --> 00:21:32,920
from occurring in the future.

273
00:21:36,100 --> 00:21:39,740
One company in California believes it has found the answer.

274
00:21:40,640 --> 00:21:49,320
Researchers at Material Technologies in Los Angeles have come up with a new way to detect

275
00:21:49,321 --> 00:21:55,480
and monitor fatigue cracks.

276
00:21:55,481 --> 00:21:57,240
Their secret weapon?

277
00:21:57,241 --> 00:22:00,560
Electrical sensors.

278
00:22:00,561 --> 00:22:02,620
Our technology is really fascinating.

279
00:22:02,621 --> 00:22:04,520
It's very, very simple.

280
00:22:04,521 --> 00:22:07,980
It's identical to an EKG on your heart.

281
00:22:07,981 --> 00:22:13,220
And the way it works is that we go to what is called the fatigue critical location of

282
00:22:13,221 --> 00:22:14,220
a bridge.

283
00:22:14,221 --> 00:22:17,980
If you have a little crack in a fatigue critical location, you've got a problem.

284
00:22:17,981 --> 00:22:23,980
First, the team mounts and preps two sensors, one on an area under the bridge that has a

285
00:22:23,981 --> 00:22:27,900
crack and one nearby that is crack free.

286
00:22:27,901 --> 00:22:32,980
As a crack changes in size under stress, the sensors will detect minute electrical impulses.

287
00:22:32,981 --> 00:22:36,780
This is the potentiostat data length.

288
00:22:36,781 --> 00:22:41,900
This actually applies the voltage and reads back the current.

289
00:22:41,901 --> 00:22:46,500
This is the brains of the operation.

290
00:22:46,501 --> 00:22:50,640
The sensors detect the amount of stress being put on the cracks from the traffic that goes

291
00:22:50,641 --> 00:22:53,160
over the bridge.

292
00:22:53,161 --> 00:22:59,720
That data is transmitted back to a computer at the company's offices, where it's monitored.

293
00:22:59,721 --> 00:23:04,860
red elongation of a spike represents a crack that's growing.

294
00:23:04,861 --> 00:23:10,500
Out in the real world, how this helps is that we've got lots of bridges out there that have

295
00:23:10,501 --> 00:23:12,300
aged and have fatigue cracks.

296
00:23:12,301 --> 00:23:17,320
And so this actually gives us a diagnostic tool by putting those sensors on there for

297
00:23:17,321 --> 00:23:20,780
us to be able to determine which cracks are growing and which ones are not, which ones

298
00:23:20,781 --> 00:23:24,400
need to be repaired and which ones we can leave.

299
00:23:24,401 --> 00:23:36,640
The collapse of Bridge 9340 in Minneapolis has created a higher awareness of aging bridge infrastructures in America and made finding solutions a new national priority.

300
00:23:38,020 --> 00:23:50,020
In the meantime, the search continues in Minneapolis, a thorough and tedious process, digging through the wreckage for clues to what brought Bridge 9340 down.

301
00:23:50,021 --> 00:23:59,360
The 144,000 cars and trucks that traveled across the Minneapolis Bridge each day in 2007

302
00:23:59,361 --> 00:24:05,160
represented twice the projected traffic flow planned for when the bridge was built in 1967.

303
00:24:07,400 --> 00:24:10,380
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

304
00:24:14,320 --> 00:24:16,160
September 11, 1982.

305
00:24:17,060 --> 00:24:19,000
At an air show in Mannheim, Germany.

306
00:24:20,020 --> 00:24:24,120
A US Army helicopter suddenly plummets from the sky.

307
00:24:24,121 --> 00:24:27,820
It crashes to the ground and bursts into flames.

308
00:24:27,821 --> 00:24:32,340
All 46 people on board perish.

309
00:24:32,341 --> 00:24:34,740
The mission of the aircraft on the day of the accident

310
00:24:34,741 --> 00:24:39,580
was to support a parachute drop, to support the 375th birthday

311
00:24:39,581 --> 00:24:42,580
of the Mannheim community.

312
00:24:42,581 --> 00:24:45,280
The aircraft took off and was climbing to an assigned

313
00:24:45,281 --> 00:24:48,820
altitude, which would allow for the paratroopers to jump.

314
00:24:48,821 --> 00:24:53,720
They climbed to about 10,000 feet, and then they started coming back down.

315
00:24:54,240 --> 00:24:58,200
And from what we know is that the second Chinook that was on the ground

316
00:24:58,201 --> 00:25:01,460
called them and asked, why are you coming back down?

317
00:25:01,620 --> 00:25:03,980
You haven't dropped your parachute teams yet.

318
00:25:06,360 --> 00:25:11,340
They landed with basically over 200 Gs of force, and it was completely unsurvileable.

319
00:25:11,840 --> 00:25:17,300
What remained of the Chinook 47C helicopter was a pile of burned and twisted metal.

320
00:25:18,820 --> 00:25:26,660
Officials from the Army's aviation headquarters in Fort Rucker, Alabama, rushed to the scene to investigate the events that led to this tragedy.

321
00:25:30,320 --> 00:25:36,160
Eyewitnesses reported that the Chinook's rear pylon and rotor detached from the aircraft during its descent.

322
00:25:37,040 --> 00:25:39,700
It dropped to the ground from 500 feet.

323
00:25:41,640 --> 00:25:46,675
How could this have happened to the esteemed aerial workhorse that continues to serve the U.S.

324
00:25:46,676 --> 00:25:47,800
military to this day?

325
00:25:48,820 --> 00:25:55,820
Since its debut in 1961, the Chinook had had an impeccable safety record.

326
00:25:55,821 --> 00:25:58,788
The Chinook is the Army's heavy lift platform.

327
00:25:58,789 --> 00:26:01,820
It has a useful payload of about 20,000 pounds.

328
00:26:01,821 --> 00:26:08,820
That payload can consist of beams, bullets, soldiers, putting critical supplies at the right place.

329
00:26:08,821 --> 00:26:16,335
If you want to move your organization and you want to do it quickly, well then you ask for a Chinook because you can get a lot of guys on it and you can haul some

330
00:26:16,336 --> 00:26:17,820
equipment while you're doing it.

331
00:26:17,821 --> 00:26:20,320
How are you doing?

332
00:26:20,321 --> 00:26:23,900
Could pilot error explain the cause of the crash?

333
00:26:23,901 --> 00:26:26,100
Or was the disaster triggered by a mechanical failure?

334
00:26:29,020 --> 00:26:32,540
The Chinook helicopter is equipped with two engines.

335
00:26:32,541 --> 00:26:37,340
The engines are connected by a combining transmission.

336
00:26:37,341 --> 00:26:39,580
The engines, through the combining transmission,

337
00:26:39,581 --> 00:26:42,400
run drive shafts that control the synchronized rotation

338
00:26:42,401 --> 00:26:44,100
of the rotors.

339
00:26:44,101 --> 00:26:46,300
This allows the rotor blades to pass by each other

340
00:26:46,301 --> 00:26:48,800
without making contact.

341
00:26:48,801 --> 00:26:51,740
The tandem action provides both the lift and thrust

342
00:26:51,741 --> 00:26:53,560
of the aircraft.

343
00:26:53,561 --> 00:26:56,820
Investigators hope to find clues to the crash in the engine

344
00:26:56,821 --> 00:26:59,700
and rotor hardware.

345
00:26:59,701 --> 00:27:02,240
But at the crash site, few parts of the Chinook

346
00:27:02,241 --> 00:27:03,800
remained intact.

347
00:27:03,801 --> 00:27:06,880
Roughly 85% of the aircraft was consumed

348
00:27:06,881 --> 00:27:09,300
by the crash and the fire.

349
00:27:09,301 --> 00:27:13,440
Still, they were able to recover one key piece of evidence.

350
00:27:13,441 --> 00:27:15,180
The team on the preliminary investigation

351
00:27:15,181 --> 00:27:20,100
was able to see some heat stress on the pinion bearing that failed.

352
00:27:20,600 --> 00:27:22,820
That part hadn't been totally consumed by the fire,

353
00:27:22,960 --> 00:27:24,420
so they were able to further pursue that.

354
00:27:25,620 --> 00:27:28,640
The pinion bearing was part of the forward transmission.

355
00:27:30,580 --> 00:27:35,560
So the first indication of a bearing looking like it's overstressed

356
00:27:35,561 --> 00:27:38,460
is kind of like a smoking gun.

357
00:27:38,461 --> 00:27:42,500
You're looking for something like that to tell you where the failure occurred.

358
00:27:42,501 --> 00:27:46,500
Small jets in the transmission, known as oil ports,

359
00:27:46,501 --> 00:27:49,500
are used to lubricate the pinion bearings.

360
00:27:49,501 --> 00:27:52,500
Army investigators discovered that something had clogged the

361
00:27:52,501 --> 00:27:55,500
jets, preventing the oil from flowing to the bearing.

362
00:27:55,501 --> 00:27:58,500
They were able to determine that it was an organic material

363
00:27:58,501 --> 00:28:01,500
similar to very fine walnut shells.

364
00:28:01,501 --> 00:28:05,500
The pieces of the puzzle began to fall into place.

365
00:28:05,501 --> 00:28:08,500
For years, the Army had used ground walnut shells to clean

366
00:28:08,501 --> 00:28:11,500
the Chinook parts.

367
00:28:11,501 --> 00:28:14,320
These finely ground particles were non-abrasive to metal.

368
00:28:15,660 --> 00:28:19,460
The wool nut material was flushed through the engine parts using high-pressure air.

369
00:28:20,080 --> 00:28:25,420
It was a matter that they used at that time to clean cases, transmission cases,

370
00:28:25,660 --> 00:28:28,360
kind of like sandblasting but using that organic matter.

371
00:28:28,660 --> 00:28:32,600
That at the time was a very effective means for cleaning a multitude of aviation parts.

372
00:28:33,320 --> 00:28:37,840
The conclusion of the investigation showed that the grid that did not get flushed

373
00:28:37,841 --> 00:28:44,600
caused the whole port to clog, which started the chain event for the crash itself.

374
00:28:45,500 --> 00:28:51,280
The oil jet, clogged with the ground walnut shells, caused the pinion bearing to seize and then fail.

375
00:28:52,740 --> 00:28:58,780
This caused the front rotor to desynchronize with its aft counterpart, and the two became enmeshed.

376
00:28:59,540 --> 00:29:01,560
The blades were hitting each other as they rotated.

377
00:29:01,561 --> 00:29:09,160
During the Army's two-month investigation, the entire fleet of Chinook 47Cs was grounded.

378
00:29:11,220 --> 00:29:14,820
Investigators discovered that one other Chinook had walnut grit in its engine,

379
00:29:15,260 --> 00:29:20,100
and military officials decided to end its use and find alternative cleaning engines.

380
00:29:20,840 --> 00:29:25,780
We use solvents, chemicals that are in liquid form that would not cause a blockage.

381
00:29:25,781 --> 00:29:31,860
Since the accident in 1982, there have been other safety improvements made to the aircraft.

382
00:29:32,660 --> 00:29:40,640
We've added pressure and temperature sensors, debris screen latches that would give an indication

383
00:29:40,641 --> 00:29:45,000
to the crew in the front or the flight engineer in the back that you could have an impending failure,

384
00:29:45,660 --> 00:29:50,520
giving him time to perform an emergency procedure that would prevent a catastrophic incident.

385
00:29:50,521 --> 00:29:57,640
During the quarter century since the tragedy in Mannheim, the Chinook has remained a valuable

386
00:29:57,641 --> 00:30:01,020
strategic asset for the U.S. Armed Forces.

387
00:30:01,021 --> 00:30:09,040
And today, over 400 Chinooks take to the skies in defense of America's interests.

388
00:30:09,041 --> 00:30:17,800
In the early 1990s, real estate tycoon Donald Trump owned two Chinook CH-47s.

389
00:30:17,801 --> 00:30:24,460
As part of Trump Air, the helicopters were used to shuttle his clients from New York City to his casinos in New Jersey.

390
00:30:25,600 --> 00:30:30,760
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

391
00:30:32,960 --> 00:30:38,880
We're getting suspicious calls of some sort of explosion, unknown if it's occurring on the freeway or off the freeway.

392
00:30:38,881 --> 00:30:41,120
Looks like we have a large fire at the maze.

393
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:43,940
Time for possibly a tanker.

394
00:30:43,941 --> 00:30:48,620
Yeah, the transition to Southbound 880 is fully engulfed.

395
00:30:50,340 --> 00:30:54,740
On April 29, 2007, at 3.27 a.m.,

396
00:30:54,741 --> 00:30:58,720
a tanker truck heading south on Interstate 880 in Oakland, California,

397
00:30:59,500 --> 00:31:03,620
crashed into a guardrail, flipped over, and burst into flames.

398
00:31:04,520 --> 00:31:09,240
It was carrying a full load, 8,600 gallons of gasoline.

399
00:31:10,240 --> 00:31:12,580
15 minutes after the gasoline exploded,

400
00:31:13,200 --> 00:31:16,360
the I-580 overpass above the truck collapsed.

401
00:31:16,840 --> 00:31:18,640
All available units, code 3.

402
00:31:20,440 --> 00:31:23,300
This alarming disaster was a shocking wake-up call

403
00:31:23,301 --> 00:31:24,520
for state government officials

404
00:31:24,521 --> 00:31:26,880
and California transportation personnel.

405
00:31:28,300 --> 00:31:30,620
I wanted to come here personally

406
00:31:30,621 --> 00:31:34,560
to see the damage that was caused here

407
00:31:34,561 --> 00:31:37,120
because of this accident that happened early this morning.

408
00:31:37,121 --> 00:31:41,800
The 160-foot section of the I-580 overpass was completely destroyed.

409
00:31:44,640 --> 00:31:47,480
The disaster brought traffic to a dead stop.

410
00:31:48,700 --> 00:31:50,840
I declare the state of emergency today.

411
00:31:51,280 --> 00:31:56,400
The state of emergency was used so we can cut through all the red tape and cut through the bureaucracy.

412
00:31:57,260 --> 00:32:02,520
The section of the Oakland freeway system where the accident occurred is called the MacArthur Maze.

413
00:32:03,420 --> 00:32:05,840
Three major interstate routes converge here,

414
00:32:06,020 --> 00:32:08,620
on their way to the Bay Bridge, heading into San Francisco.

415
00:32:09,620 --> 00:32:14,600
There are about 280,000 vehicles that go across that bridge every day,

416
00:32:15,140 --> 00:32:18,060
so it is really a lifeline for the community.

417
00:32:18,960 --> 00:32:21,700
Learning the cause of this freeway failure was important,

418
00:32:22,420 --> 00:32:24,620
but the California Department of Transportation

419
00:32:24,621 --> 00:32:27,220
had a very immediate problem to deal with first.

420
00:32:28,300 --> 00:32:30,780
How to reroute the enormous volume of traffic.

421
00:32:30,781 --> 00:32:38,880
We have a large number of variable message signs, highway advisory radios that we were

422
00:32:38,881 --> 00:32:46,280
able to activate virtually immediately for an incident like this.

423
00:32:46,281 --> 00:32:49,900
Every day the freeway was out of commission caused commuter times to increase more than

424
00:32:49,901 --> 00:32:55,740
67 percent, with a financial impact of millions of dollars.

425
00:32:55,741 --> 00:33:00,020
Caltrans designers and project managers had to quickly formulate a plan of action.

426
00:33:00,780 --> 00:33:07,260
What happened over the next few hours and days really amounted to doing investigative work.

427
00:33:07,261 --> 00:33:13,460
First of all, we were trying to make sure that it was safe for our investigative teams to get onto the site.

428
00:33:14,300 --> 00:33:16,600
Right now we're underneath the 880 connector,

429
00:33:17,160 --> 00:33:20,880
and this is the structure on which the tanker truck was driving

430
00:33:20,881 --> 00:33:24,480
and on which the other structure, the upper structure, fell.

431
00:33:25,740 --> 00:33:29,560
This structure had the burning fuel on the deck from the tanker.

432
00:33:30,080 --> 00:33:33,200
There was a drain in the deck, so there was also burning fuel down here.

433
00:33:33,720 --> 00:33:38,320
The Caltrans team had to remove the collapsed section of the 580 overpass safely

434
00:33:38,321 --> 00:33:42,200
and make sure the structural integrity of the I-880 freeway,

435
00:33:42,560 --> 00:33:45,420
where the truck exploded, wasn't compromised in any way.

436
00:33:47,000 --> 00:33:50,840
We came up with a shoring or false work system

437
00:33:50,841 --> 00:33:52,860
that we installed underneath the 880 structure.

438
00:33:53,320 --> 00:33:54,920
Basically, we built a bridge under the bridge.

439
00:33:55,740 --> 00:34:01,620
Caltrans also enlisted the help of an engineering firm to analyze the concrete on the I-880 deck.

440
00:34:02,280 --> 00:34:06,640
The big questions were what was the condition of the concrete that was still remaining

441
00:34:06,641 --> 00:34:13,560
and whether or not, for example, the concrete deck or supporting columns needed substantial repair.

442
00:34:14,900 --> 00:34:18,080
Engineers used several methods to test the concrete for damage.

443
00:34:18,620 --> 00:34:21,860
One was decidedly low-tech, yet highly effective,

444
00:34:23,020 --> 00:34:25,120
wrapping the surface of the concrete with a hammer.

445
00:34:25,740 --> 00:34:32,760
It's got a nice high-pitched solid ring to it. Damaged concrete would it would be

446
00:34:32,761 --> 00:34:38,640
more of a thud. I mean not nearly a high-pitched ring. The core samples taken

447
00:34:38,641 --> 00:34:42,000
from the disaster site were tested in the laboratory for their compression

448
00:34:42,001 --> 00:34:50,180
strength. These are placed in a test apparatus and load is applied until the

449
00:34:50,181 --> 00:34:55,020
concrete sample breaks and that's called the concrete compressive strength. From

450
00:34:55,021 --> 00:35:00,580
From that we can understand whether or not there's a lot of internal damage to it.

451
00:35:00,581 --> 00:35:06,460
Engineers also subjected the concrete to a process called petrographic examination.

452
00:35:06,461 --> 00:35:14,400
What happens is in the laboratory they slice or cut the core in half, so you have two pieces.

453
00:35:14,401 --> 00:35:19,100
They polish the halves very smoothly.

454
00:35:19,101 --> 00:35:23,820
The petrographers examined the polished concrete under microscopes to look for any cracks and

455
00:35:23,821 --> 00:35:29,920
color changes that might be imperceptible to the naked eye.

456
00:35:29,921 --> 00:35:34,140
When all the tests had been done, lab and field experts determined that the deck of

457
00:35:34,141 --> 00:35:39,000
the I-880 freeway had suffered only superficial damage.

458
00:35:39,001 --> 00:35:44,500
Only eight days after the collapse, Caltrans allowed traffic back on the I-880.

459
00:35:44,501 --> 00:35:48,940
Now 35,000 cars a day were on the road again.

460
00:35:48,941 --> 00:35:54,000
45,000 more commuters would still have to wait until the I-580 overpass was rebuilt.

461
00:35:55,740 --> 00:35:59,320
Caltrans put the rebuild of the overpass on an accelerated schedule.

462
00:36:00,560 --> 00:36:05,820
First, working with round-the-clock construction crews, they erected new concrete support columns.

463
00:36:06,660 --> 00:36:12,780
And since the steel beam capping them had collapsed during the tanker fire, they added a new beam made of concrete.

464
00:36:12,781 --> 00:36:19,780
At the same time, ironworkers were fabricating the steel girders to support the overpass.

465
00:36:19,781 --> 00:36:26,780
If you look at the structural steel girders that are on some of these other existing spans,

466
00:36:26,781 --> 00:36:32,780
you'll see a lot of vertical plates that are welded on, and those are individually welded,

467
00:36:32,781 --> 00:36:35,033
and that takes a lot of fabrication time.

468
00:36:35,034 --> 00:36:37,780
So what we did on the design of the new girders is

469
00:36:37,781 --> 00:36:43,320
is we minimized that and tried to minimize the amount of welding that would be required

470
00:36:43,321 --> 00:36:46,600
to fabricate these girders so that they could be fabricated much quicker.

471
00:36:48,240 --> 00:36:53,280
Once the team got the girders in place, concrete pourers prepared the deck.

472
00:36:54,160 --> 00:36:56,840
It was one of the last steps in the rebuild process.

473
00:36:58,220 --> 00:37:04,120
By May 24, 2007, Caltrans had completed the new I-580 overpass.

474
00:37:05,120 --> 00:37:09,280
Except for some final painting, the MacArthur maze was once again up and running.

475
00:37:12,180 --> 00:37:14,580
What had caused its collapse in the first place?

476
00:37:15,800 --> 00:37:18,700
Caltrans officials determined that when the tanker truck exploded,

477
00:37:19,220 --> 00:37:22,500
the temperature of the fire reached 3,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

478
00:37:23,260 --> 00:37:27,880
This extreme heat melted the steel girders and caused the concrete overpass to fall.

479
00:37:28,740 --> 00:37:31,660
Could any designer have anticipated such a freakish event?

480
00:37:32,480 --> 00:37:36,540
From an engineer's point of view, you can't think of everything, no matter how hard you try.

481
00:37:36,960 --> 00:37:41,320
And even if you do try and think of everything, that drives up the costs of the project.

482
00:37:41,760 --> 00:37:47,360
So there is a trade-off between degree of safety and the amount of money that you're going to spend on an individual bridge.

483
00:37:48,540 --> 00:37:52,680
If an engineering disaster can have a happy ending, here was one.

484
00:37:53,180 --> 00:37:54,640
No one had been killed.

485
00:37:54,641 --> 00:38:03,060
And through persistent and coordinated efforts, Caltrans had rebuilt a vital overpass in an astounding 26 days.

486
00:38:05,980 --> 00:38:11,895
By completing the construction of the I-580 overpass more than 30 days early, the C.C.

487
00:38:11,896 --> 00:38:14,440
Myers Company won a $5 million bonus.

488
00:38:15,360 --> 00:38:20,440
Its original bid for the repair work was only $867,075.

489
00:38:20,441 --> 00:38:25,520
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

490
00:38:28,280 --> 00:38:32,000
Imagine your house, your neighborhood, your entire town,

491
00:38:32,680 --> 00:38:36,440
flooded by a torrent of mud, gushing from a crack in the earth.

492
00:38:37,460 --> 00:38:41,620
This frightening scenario happened in 2006 in Sidoaro,

493
00:38:42,080 --> 00:38:43,960
a town in East Java, Indonesia.

494
00:38:44,880 --> 00:38:47,440
The mud began its assault on May 29th.

495
00:38:47,441 --> 00:38:52,820
Over the course of the next few months, the rate at which mud came up to the surface increased

496
00:38:52,821 --> 00:38:59,000
dramatically and has persisted for a couple of years without any signs of slowing down.

497
00:38:59,001 --> 00:39:03,440
The phenomenon is called a mud volcano.

498
00:39:03,441 --> 00:39:09,860
And it has unleashed its slow motion destruction over 1,500 acres, destroying villages, factories

499
00:39:09,861 --> 00:39:11,860
and fields.

500
00:39:11,861 --> 00:39:17,140
30,000 people have been displaced.

501
00:39:17,141 --> 00:39:19,400
There's lots of seepage from over there.

502
00:39:19,401 --> 00:39:21,360
This area was all paddy fields.

503
00:39:21,361 --> 00:39:23,740
There were paddy fields all the way down there.

504
00:39:23,741 --> 00:39:29,820
The mud that's erupting is also hot, so it's not easy to recolonize or resettle the area

505
00:39:29,821 --> 00:39:31,320
that's been erupting.

506
00:39:31,321 --> 00:39:37,000
And some of the mud is now going into rivers and streams and then into the ocean.

507
00:39:37,001 --> 00:39:42,020
A mud volcano is a natural phenomenon that originates deep within the earth, where deposits

508
00:39:42,021 --> 00:39:45,700
of mud exist.

509
00:39:45,701 --> 00:39:51,920
activity, compaction of sediments, or an accumulation of hydrocarbon gases force changes in the

510
00:39:51,921 --> 00:39:53,280
pressure of the mud.

511
00:39:53,281 --> 00:39:57,700
These pressure changes propel the mud upward to the surface where

512
00:39:57,701 --> 00:39:58,935
it's expelled.

513
00:39:58,936 --> 00:40:05,200
The Indonesian mud volcano is one of the world's largest, shooting over

514
00:40:05,201 --> 00:40:06,623
46 feet into the air.

515
00:40:06,624 --> 00:40:10,960
Real volcanoes erupt in part because they're gases in the magma,

516
00:40:10,961 --> 00:40:14,640
the molten rock. Those gases make the magma light and light things go up.

517
00:40:15,360 --> 00:40:18,741
And mud volcanoes contain lots of dissolved gases too.

518
00:40:18,742 --> 00:40:21,120
And so these gases form bubbles as the

519
00:40:21,121 --> 00:40:25,200
mud rises to the surface. It makes it lighter and that helps drive the eruption.

520
00:40:26,000 --> 00:40:29,083
What triggered this rare occurrence in East Java?

521
00:40:29,084 --> 00:40:31,600
Some experts believe it was caused by an

522
00:40:31,601 --> 00:40:37,200
engineering disaster because the site of the eruption was near a natural gas drilling well.

523
00:40:37,201 --> 00:40:44,820
Geologists who have studied the mud volcano first-hand concluded that the Lepindo Brontus

524
00:40:44,821 --> 00:40:48,180
Company was drilling to a depth of more than 9,000 feet.

525
00:40:48,181 --> 00:40:51,744
And the depth at which the muds are erupting is similar to that.

526
00:40:51,745 --> 00:40:53,080
And we know how deep the

527
00:40:53,081 --> 00:40:58,500
muds are coming from by looking at the types of muds that are erupted compared to what the

528
00:40:58,501 --> 00:41:01,314
drillers pull up in the well when they're drilling.

529
00:41:01,315 --> 00:41:03,300
According to the geological experts,

530
00:41:03,301 --> 00:41:09,540
On May 28th, the day before the mud began erupting, the Natural Gas Company had drilled

531
00:41:09,541 --> 00:41:10,855
to 9,297 feet.

532
00:41:10,856 --> 00:41:17,620
They pulled the drill bit out of the hole, and this probably triggered a

533
00:41:17,621 --> 00:41:23,401
catastrophic kick of hydrogen sulfide gas and water from the surrounding rocks.

534
00:41:23,402 --> 00:41:24,102
The water

535
00:41:24,103 --> 00:41:26,697
and gas began filtering up through the wellbore.

536
00:41:26,698 --> 00:41:29,060
To prevent the toxic mixture from escaping,

537
00:41:29,061 --> 00:41:30,857
the company capped the well.

538
00:41:30,858 --> 00:41:34,900
This could have created an increase in pressure inside the hole

539
00:41:34,901 --> 00:41:36,433
that fractured the rocks.

540
00:41:36,434 --> 00:41:40,420
The fractures allowed the mud to travel to the surface and erupt.

541
00:41:41,080 --> 00:41:45,351
The actual mud volcano was 590 feet away from the well.

542
00:41:45,352 --> 00:41:48,380
But could the eruption have been caused

543
00:41:48,381 --> 00:41:50,119
instead by natural forces?

544
00:41:50,120 --> 00:41:54,800
Such is the assertion of the Indonesian drilling company, which claims

545
00:41:54,801 --> 00:41:58,620
that an earthquake two days before the disaster must have been responsible.

546
00:42:00,220 --> 00:42:04,940
Many institutions did some studies, many geologists, engineers.

547
00:42:05,780 --> 00:42:09,360
They tend to say that this is natural disaster

548
00:42:09,361 --> 00:42:14,820
rather than mechanical or technical problems of the drilling.

549
00:42:15,720 --> 00:42:19,780
But engineers both in Indonesia and around the world have disputed this claim,

550
00:42:20,500 --> 00:42:24,340
finding that the earthquake was too distant, 300 miles from the eruption.

551
00:42:24,800 --> 00:42:29,420
and it's 6.3 on the Richter scale, too weak to cause this kind of mass destruction.

552
00:42:31,440 --> 00:42:34,700
Indonesia is probably the most seismically active region on Earth,

553
00:42:34,920 --> 00:42:36,380
and so there are lots and lots of earthquakes.

554
00:42:37,200 --> 00:42:39,480
And there would have been many times in the last 30 years

555
00:42:39,481 --> 00:42:42,220
that an earthquake might have triggered an eruption, and it never did.

556
00:42:42,560 --> 00:42:45,680
And some of the shaking caused by those other earthquakes is much, much bigger,

557
00:42:45,980 --> 00:42:48,580
as much as 100 to 1,000 times bigger shaking.

558
00:42:50,160 --> 00:42:53,840
The government of Indonesia brought in engineers from around the world

559
00:42:53,841 --> 00:42:55,440
to work on stopping the mud flow.

560
00:42:56,800 --> 00:43:00,580
One unconventional method has been to drop concrete balls

561
00:43:00,581 --> 00:43:03,800
held together by chains into the crater to plug it up.

562
00:43:06,220 --> 00:43:08,660
It's the first time anything like this has been attempted.

563
00:43:09,700 --> 00:43:12,260
When we start this project, basically,

564
00:43:12,261 --> 00:43:17,240
there's a lot of concern from society in general

565
00:43:17,241 --> 00:43:20,400
and, in fact, from some scientific community

566
00:43:20,401 --> 00:43:23,440
that this could lead to things like the balls

567
00:43:23,441 --> 00:43:24,520
and become projectile.

568
00:43:25,200 --> 00:43:27,500
And so far, thanks God, it's not happened.

569
00:43:28,620 --> 00:43:31,000
Despite the effort to cork the source of the mud,

570
00:43:31,620 --> 00:43:33,760
the output of the volcano has increased

571
00:43:33,761 --> 00:43:37,040
from 5,000 cubic meters a day in 2006

572
00:43:37,041 --> 00:43:40,260
to 150,000 cubic meters today.

573
00:43:41,680 --> 00:43:44,900
Some enterprising farmers are harvesting salt from the mud

574
00:43:44,901 --> 00:43:46,560
and selling it at the markets.

575
00:43:48,180 --> 00:43:50,700
And the phenomenon presents an exceptional chance

576
00:43:50,701 --> 00:43:52,840
to study mud volcanoes at close range.

577
00:43:53,440 --> 00:44:00,160
It would be a shame if we don't learn anything from this disaster.

578
00:44:00,161 --> 00:44:04,540
And here we have a well that goes all the way down, and you have a special opportunity,

579
00:44:04,541 --> 00:44:09,360
maybe a unique opportunity, to look at the complete history of a mud volcano, from before

580
00:44:09,361 --> 00:44:13,860
its initiation, through its evolution, and finally when it ends.

581
00:44:13,861 --> 00:44:19,000
The mud volcano shows no signs of abating, so scientists may be able to observe it for

582
00:44:19,001 --> 00:44:22,200
years and perhaps even decades to come.

